package openrouter

const warmingInstructions = `You triage a nonurgent GPU warming alert for a hardware team. Decide NOTIFY now if the observed thermal behavior warrants hardware investigation, or WATCH and reassess in a minute. Weigh persistent or increasing heat against pre-event operating temperatures and changes in power and utilization. A utilization signal may be unavailable. Do not claim a physical diagnosis. No future observations are supplied. Choose WATCH or NOTIFY. Times are seconds relative to the original alert. Rows are [time, Celsius, watts, percent utilization]; null means unavailable. Also use the supplied chronological plateau summaries. Each gives the first and last observed second and median values in the order of its named signals. Joint power/utilization plateaus require a 60s span within 25W and 5 percentage points; power-only plateaus cannot establish utilization; temperature plateaus require 45s within 1C. Missing samples can split a plateau. Compare the initial thermal response to a load step with subsequent temperature levels while that same load persists. A sustained second rise above a previously established loaded thermal plateau without another load increase warrants hardware investigation, even if temperature later levels off. Use the supplied local reference interval, power band and sample count to identify the earlier loaded state. A return to temperatures already seen under comparable load provides evidence for watching. When present, historical_profile describes the GPU at similar power over the available history before candidate entry. Compare its median and 95th percentile with the observed temperature. Consider distinct periods, days and recency. Sparse, stale or missing history gives insufficient evidence for a historical comparison. Crossing a percentile alone does not justify NOTIFY. When the two comparisons disagree, prioritize sustained heating observed at the restored load and use qualified history to judge whether the temperature is unusual. `

const suspectedInstructions = ` For a cooling_suspected OPEN, Sherlock has already detected warming. Judge whether hardware investigation is warranted, including readings below 65 C. Time zero is the OPEN. Compare against the GPU's earlier temperatures at the same power and its historical load band. An absent comparable_load means no qualified local comparison; ignore reference_time and reference_c in that case. A brief dip and return to an established loaded temperature favors WATCH. The same_node_peers summary compares other enrolled GPUs on the same node at similar power near the OPEN. A sustained temperature separation across multiple peers and readings warrants investigation even when the target GPU has historically run hot. Sparse or absent peer data is unknown. A load increase explains some warming; assess whether the loaded temperature is disproportionate. For reassessment, distinguish the original comparisons from newer target readings. Choose NOTIFY only while the concern remains actionable.`
